{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1470232-v5-your-beckoning","id":1470232,"title":"[V5] Your Beckoning","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-12-11T12:13:52-08:00","id":1470232,"title":"[V5] Your Beckoning","content":"The Beckoning is at the same time a crucial plot device of V5 and a still vague event, at least in its scope and functioning. <br /> <br /> In my first V5 Chronicle, set in Krakow, characters were employed by the Tzimisce Voivode after the mass disappearance of Tzimisce elders: she feared a coup by the Old Clan, whereas they were summoned by the Ante. <br /> <br /> How have you used the Beckoning in your Chronicles? <br /> <br /> I've imagined the Beckoning as this subtle descent into madness that suddenyl bursts into Elders' minds: they flee East as not-totally conscious, often forcing their ghouls or kin to take them there. When they arrive close enough, the call becomes too strong to be resisted: they move around like demented demigods, following the will of their Ancients into some battlegrounds, often the sites of burial of artifacts or relics. <br /> <br /> When some of those elders diablerize enough rivals or underlings, they are able to regain a degree of autonomy: sometimes they try to make sense of what has happened and contact their former domains, sometimes they are too degraded for this, and engage in warlordism, creating Ancestor Cults and Gehenna Cults. <br /> <br /> And while this happens, fanatic Sabbats come like locusts and desperate Ashirra try to make sense of what is happening, and defend their Domains from the invasion of these swarms of eldritch monsters.<br /> <br /> As more and more Ancients come near their awakening, the Gehenna War becomes wider, ranging from preColumbian ruins to Russia, from Greek, Etruscan and Roman catacombs to Africa.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12590,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","user":"Manfr","date":". 12-11-2021, 03:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-12T10:26:41-08:00","id":1470286,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I haven\u2019t used the Beckoning at all. I\u2019m running V20 and other than a couple of 7th Gen reclusive elders the entire Kindred population including the Prince are 8th Gen or higher.<br /> <br /> The Beckoning in V5 works because it\u2019s presumed a fairly sizable time jump from c. 2004 to 2015-ish and the premise is that it occurred years ago and so the vast majority being Gen 10+ is just the norm for cities now.<br /> <br /> Blowing up an existing campaign by arbitrarily writing out every 7-9th Gen in the setting out by contrast sounds like some of the most atrocious plot development one could imagine; akin to actually listening to the advice in Time of Thin Blood telling you that, regardless of your campaign, you should institute the Week of Nightmares immediately and kill off most of the Ravnos no matter how disruptive it would actually be\u2026 because metaplot somehow trumps good storytelling or something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-14T06:27:11-08:00","id":1470425,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris24601</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1470232-v5-your-beckoning?p=1470286#post1470286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I haven\u2019t used the Beckoning at all. I\u2019m running V20 and other than a couple of 7th Gen reclusive elders the entire Kindred population including the Prince are 8th Gen or higher.<br /> <br /> The Beckoning in V5 works because it\u2019s presumed a fairly sizable time jump from c. 2004 to 2015-ish and the premise is that it occurred years ago and so the vast majority being Gen 10+ is just the norm for cities now.<br /> <br /> Blowing up an existing campaign by arbitrarily writing out every 7-9th Gen in the setting out by contrast sounds like some of the most atrocious plot development one could imagine; akin to actually listening to the advice in Time of Thin Blood telling you that, regardless of your campaign, you should institute the Week of Nightmares immediately and kill off most of the Ravnos no matter how disruptive it would actually be\u2026 because metaplot somehow trumps good storytelling or something.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, that was very poor advice: metaplot events should be used to open possibilities for the characters, not to restrict them. The same should be true for the Beckoning.<br /> <br /> Some plot hooks that come to my mind:<ul><li><b>MMIA (Mawla Missing in Action): </b>A Mentor or Sire of one of the characters suddenly disappears. He leaves behind notes, scribbles and specimens that suggest a sudden breakdown: visionary paintings, smashed artifacts, messy rooms. All of a sudden, a power vacuum emerges: will the characters seize the chance to inherit its power, or will they investigate its whereabouts, with the risk of losing ground to hungry rivals?</li> </ul><ul><li><b>The Dinasty: </b>In this case, the Mentor or Sire of one of the characters has not disappeared - it has willingly joined the Gehenna Crusade, and now expects its Blood not only to defend Domain, but also to provide for its logistics and necessities. With time, its requests become increasingly bizarre, taxing and inhumane: are the characters going to comply?</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Murder Scene:</b> the Prince and Primogen are no more! They have convened to discuss the strange sightings and nightmarish visions that are affecting them, but the Will of the Ancients has manifested itself, and one or more of them has attacked the others. The city is ripe for taking - but visions are now spreading: what do the Ancients want, from the Kindred of this city?</li> </ul><b>\u200b</b><ul><li><b>Migration Patterns:</b> The city suddenly finds itself on a migration pattern by rival Elders, who start stalking Fledgelings to sate their Hunger while those strong enough are Dominated, Blood Bonded and drafted into their private armies. What starts as a series of black ops and extractions, slowly degenerates into open war, as the Elders fight about ... what? Is there a sleeping Ancient? A noddist artifact? What are the Kindred in the city going to do, are they taking sides or will they try to organize a resistance?</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Business for the Circulatory System: </b>The Beckoning screams in the ears of the Elders, and only Diablerie seems powerful enough to stop it. Thin Bloods, Caitiffs and Anarchs are all in danger: the characters get involved when some Neonate they know is captured and smuggled as food for the Elders.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Taking the War Home: </b>An Elder suddenly comes back from the War, a feverish fervor in his mind and words, spreading the gospel of the Ancients' rise and gathering a Gehenna cult, promising power, bloood and artifacts to those who will answer the call.</li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12590-manfr","name":"Manfr","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"449"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-14T07:42:07-08:00","id":1470433,"title":"","content":"I prefer to run the Beckoning as a malady that may or may not be linked to the Ancients. A feverish devotion to Them is interesting, but can only remain mysterious so long, eventually it has to either reveal their agendas or reveal that they have no agenda, since this isn't the Jyhad anymore, but a full blown war. And then it is just high level Dominate one way or another. Not that I'm not in favor of revealing the Ancients' agendas, but not in a way that robs the mystery of the Beckoning itself.<br /> <br /> My idea is a feverish state resembling one or many Dissociative disorders. The elders taken enter a fugue state and wander around, taking on new personas part or all the time, oblivious to what's truly happening to them. The source of this state cannot be tracked, and even the Ancients may be just using those poor bastards instead of causing or directing their fugues, or even be on the same state themselves. No one has the means to know.<br /> <br /> One possibility is that those who actually ally with an Ancient after that do so not out of feverish devotion, but payment for the service of being taken care in the throes of the Beckoning, however the Ancient <i>seems</i> to help the elder. Are they seemingly cured? Are they cared for while in the gripes of the Beckoning until it fades? Are they given something to direct or soothe the fugue state, passing for treatment while it can't be cured?<br /> <br /> Another thing is that by now the Beckoning should be something already observed to a great extent. Some victims are coming home or at least their cities received news on them. Scholars are studying them and their behavior on the field, and in some cases in labs. They have been talked to and their answers recorded or, at least, turned into gossip and legend. Some developed fully new identities, maybe multiple ones, following their own schemes elsewhere. Others just behave erratically in fugue, alternating with lucid states when they have no idea of what they had been doing and unable to go back home. Some behave in fugue in unexplained but purposeful ways, enacting strange rituals or building weird structures without a clear function, things they don't explain in fugue and can't explain lucid.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-14T11:40:21-08:00","id":1470455,"title":"","content":"for the campaign i'm working on, the beckoning is something that the characters (all thin bloods) may hear of, second and third hand, but won't really effect their lives (other than yet another level of background anxiety in the world.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>they/them.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27123-littlewitchmaus","name":"littlewitchmaus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27123_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"49"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}